# Findings that cannot be silenced with an inline `trivy:ignore` comment:
# trivy reports them against the file that declares the resource, which for
# these five is inside .terraform/modules/eks (upstream module code we do not
# own). Each is a deliberate, documented trade-off - see README.md and the
# comments in eks.tf / s3.tf.
#
# Usage (the file is not auto-discovered when the scan target is a subdirectory):
#   trivy config infra/envs/aws-demo --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#     --ignorefile infra/envs/aws-demo/.trivyignore \
#     --skip-dirs '**/.terraform/**'

# EKS secrets are not encrypted with a customer managed KMS key.
# A CMK is 1 USD/month plus request charges and has to be scheduled for
# deletion on every teardown. The cluster lives ~4 h and holds no production
# secret: the real ones stay in Secrets Manager and are projected by ESO.
AVD-AWS-0039

# The Kubernetes API endpoint is public.
# There is no VPN, no bastion and no private subnet in this stack (a NAT
# gateway costs more than everything else combined). `make aws-up` runs from a
# laptop with a dynamic IP.
AVD-AWS-0040

# ... and it is open to 0.0.0.0/0 by default.
# var.api_allowed_cidrs narrows it to a /32 for anyone who wants that; the
# default has to stay open or the very first `aws-up` locks the operator out.
AVD-AWS-0041

# The node security group allows unrestricted egress.
# Nodes have no NAT gateway, so they pull images from GHCR / ECR and reach the
# EKS, STS, S3 and Secrets Manager endpoints straight over the internet gateway.
# Restricting egress would mean VPC endpoints (0.01 USD/h each) or a NAT.
AVD-AWS-0104

# The upstream module ships example Kubernetes manifests under
# .terraform/modules/eks/examples/. They are never applied by this stack.
AVD-KSV-0014
AVD-KSV-0118
